it's natural that we feel worried when  there's a new threat to our health  identified Dr Dina Hinshaw held the  title of Alberta chief medical officer  of Health for almost exactly one year  before providing this update on a virus  that had locked down an entire city in  China and was spreading across the globe  the risk to albertans is currently very  low a few short months later everything  changed covid-19 had arrived in Alberta  today I'm here to announce that the  first presumptive case of covid-19 has  been detected in Alberta two weeks later  Alberta's first coveted 19 death as  heartbreaking as this news is it was  expected  this is a dangerous virus under her  guidance Alberta implemented strict  rules businesses closed students were  sent home Gatherings were prohibited and  these virtual covid-19 updates became  the norm  Dr Hinshaw became something of a rock  star hailed for her calm demeanor her  focus on science and her words of  compassion for confused and anxious  albertans now more than ever kindness  matters her face soon adorned t-shirts  along with other female Health leaders  like doctors Teresa Tam and Bonnie Henry  but Hinshaw soon found herself fighting  not only covid-19 but politics too as  cases skyrocketed and vulnerable seniors  in Long-Term Care Homes died in record  numbers a story was published using  secretly recorded audio in which Hinshaw  described an uphill battle working with  then Premier Jason Kenny's government I  am profoundly disappointed this is a  personal betrayal a third wave in Spring  2021 would see measures reintroduced but  also the availability of safe and  effective vaccines on the tail end of  that wave a decision that would turn  even the medical community against  Alberta's top doctor the open for summer  plan in which Dr Hinshaw recommended  dropping nearly all public health  measures and ditching the main pillars  of Alberta's coveted response test trace  and isolate I believe that this plan  will work for Alberta but it will take  all of us Dina Henshaw has lost the  moral authority to be our Public Health  leader we want resignation as predicted  cases swelled again in September and  October of 2021 pushing Alberta  hospitals to the brink as a last resort  The Province introduced a vaccine  passport to allow businesses to continue  operating while alienating albertans who  chose not to get vaccinated record high  hospitalizations stretched into the new  year before lower case counts finally  allowed the province to lift  restrictions in a phased approach in  June of 2022 Hinshaw's regular updates  came to an end while she made it through  the worst of the pandemic with her job  Kenny did not the UCP turned on its  leader due largely to his handling of  the pandemic and he resigned Paving the  way for Danielle Smith who immediately  promised a health care overhaul I will  be developing a new team of Public  Health advisors starting with a woman  who was the face of Alberta's journey  through covet 19.
 

